      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014. Published by Elsevier Masson SAS.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "UNLABELLED",
            "content": "Tonsillectomy is a very frequent procedure, particularly subject to postoperative pain that is considered long lasting and intense. Regulatory changes in the analgesic armamentarium, especially in children, are making the management of post-tonsillectomy pain more difficult. The present article provides an update on non-medical treatments that can be associated to classical analgesia."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "A literature review of all studies of non-medical management in tonsillectomy and postoperative analgesia, without restrictions of date, was performed on the Pubmed and Embase databases."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The treatment modalities described in the 24 selected studies could be categorized as behavioral, local, acupuncture and general."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Although most studies reported benefit in terms of post-tonsillectomy pain, the small number of studies per category precluded recommendations for current practice. It nevertheless seems useful to consider and assess these techniques, to determine whether they have a role to play in the post-tonsillectomy analgesic armamentarium."
          }
        ]
      },
